WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.711
This MATLAB coursework explores numerical methods through nine exercises.

2
00:00:05.397 --> 00:00:11.137
Linear systems, interpolation, regression, Newton iteration, smoothing, differentiation and

3
00:00:11.137 --> 00:00:14.977
quadrature connect mathematical assumptions to finite computations.

4
00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:21.332
For the function minus x to the fourth plus two x, Newton iteration gives a stationary point

5
00:00:21.332 --> 00:00:23.884
near point seven nine three seven zero one.

6
00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:29.247
The exact value is the cube root of one half, and the second derivative is negative there.

7
00:00:30.817 --> 00:00:35.720
The original plot compares a noisy sine signal and several smoothed versions, then their numerical

8
00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:36.573
derivatives.

9
00:00:37.259 --> 00:00:43.522
Window half-widths of one, five, ten and twenty correspond to windows of three, eleven, twenty-one

10
00:00:43.522 --> 00:00:45.225
and forty-one samples.

11
00:00:46.817 --> 00:00:51.845
Larger windows suppress more high-frequency noise but can also smooth away useful detail.

12
00:00:52.531 --> 00:00:55.254
The derivative makes this tradeoff especially visible.

13
00:00:55.941 --> 00:01:00.505
The coursework provides a visual comparison; it does not report a numerical smoothing error

14
00:01:00.505 --> 00:01:01.128
score.

15
00:01:02.683 --> 00:01:07.489
The trapezoidal rule approximates the integral of square root of one minus x squared from zero

16
00:01:07.489 --> 00:01:08.125
to one.

17
00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:14.381
With ten, twenty and forty subintervals, the original output approaches the exact value pi over

18
00:01:14.381 --> 00:01:14.992
four.

19
00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:21.016
Recomputing these grids gives a relative error of about point one four eight percent at forty

20
00:01:21.016 --> 00:01:22.028
subintervals.

21
00:01:22.714 --> 00:01:26.237
The observed convergence order is approximately one point five.

22
00:01:26.925 --> 00:01:31.694
The endpoint derivative is unbounded, so the usual smooth-function second-order assumption does

23
00:01:31.694 --> 00:01:32.924
not apply directly.

24
00:01:34.483 --> 00:01:39.483
The project develops a practical habit: implement the method, inspect the output, and test it

25
00:01:39.483 --> 00:01:42.133
against a mathematical reference whenever possible.

26
00:01:42.819 --> 00:01:47.763
Explore the original MATLAB figures and independent convergence check in the complete case study.
